WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.580
- Don't freak out about
Trump's classified documents,

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.950
because when the President
does it, it's not a crime.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.650
- If the president does
it, then it is not illegal.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.690
- Where have I heard that before?

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:10.890
- Well, when the president does it,

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.780
that means that it is not illegal.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.330
- Or the FBI planted the documents,

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.910
or Trump declassified
all of the documents.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.230
He just didn't tell anyone.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.780
Like so much spaghetti,
the Trump world is throwing

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.940
all kinds of defenses against the wall,

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.800
but do any of them stick?

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:26.940
So, today, we're gonna talk about

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.130
the various, completely coherent,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.710
and not at all mutually exclusive defenses

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.410
that former President Trump is asserting,

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.340
and we're also gonna talk about

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.050
three big recent developments,

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.310
namely that Trump's legal team,

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.760
after two weeks of doing
nothing, has now done something

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.650
in the form of a horribly written motion

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.120
for a special master.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.470
Additionally, one of Trump's friends

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.270
released a letter from NARA.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.774
Why he thought this was a good idea

00:00:49.774 --> 00:00:52.440
for the former president is beyond me,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.230
and finally the magistrate
judge has agreed to unseal

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.640
part of the probable cause affidavit

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.260
that gave rise to the search warrant.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.340
Now, one of Trump's defenses

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.290
for having government
records and documents

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.910
containing alleged national secrets

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.430
and nuclear weapons related
information at Mar-a-Lago

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.790
is that he had a standing
order to immediately declassify

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.070
any material brought into
the White House residence,

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.870
and then later removed to Mar-a-Lago.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.390
Now, this is not a particularly
good defense to prosecution

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.580
under the three laws cited
in the search warrant

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:18.480
that we know of,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:19.740
because none of those offenses

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.710
depend on the material
being classified itself.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.290
For example, 18 USC 1519 outlines

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.910
the federal crime of
obstruction of justice

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.670
for destroying, altering, or
falsifying government records.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.557
It's illegal to mutilate,
conceal, or alter, quote,

00:01:32.557 --> 00:01:34.980
"any record, document,
or tangible object,"

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.140
if the act is done with the intent to

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.660
impede, obstruct, or
influence an investigation.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.400
It doesn't matter if

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.160
the material itself was classified or not.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.350
The only thing that the
government has to show is that

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:49.140
someone was destroying or
altering any tangible object

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.270
for the purpose of
obstructing an investigation,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.280
but, nonetheless, let's
look into the claim that

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.300
Trump had a secret standing
order to declassify records.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.510
Rick Grenell, Trump's national
security director, said that,

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.220
quote, "There is this phony idea

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.950
that he must provide notification
for declassification,

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.120
but that's just silly.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:07.920
Who is he supposed to notify?"

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.720
Well, the answer is
he's supposed to notify

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.968
the American people and
members of the administration.

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.640
When something is declassified,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.770
that means the public is
allowed to see the information.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:17.940
The declassification system

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.890
isn't about the president's convenience.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:20.723
Glenn Gerstell,

00:02:20.723 --> 00:02:23.190
the top lawyer for the
National Security Agency

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.970
from 2015 to 2020, called
Trump's claim preposterous.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.890
He said that the information
should be logged,

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:30.120
so it could be identified.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.710
He also said that Trump should notify

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.380
the federal agencies
that use those documents,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.180
but Trump's power as commander-in-chief

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.700
gave him broad authority
to declassify records,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.400
but Trump didn't wield his
power to declassify records

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.170
in the way that past presidents did,

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.630
which is probably why we
keep hearing new theories

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.910
about how he might have
declassified information.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.110
We can learn a lot by looking at

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.480
what his predecessor did
with classified information.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.240
President Obama issued
Executive Order 13526

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.197
on December 29th, 2009.

00:02:56.197 --> 00:02:59.700
Obama's order created a
National Declassification Center

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:01.800
with a mission to declassify
government materials

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.570
and make them available to the public.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.790
To that end, the Obama
order established that

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.340
no records may remain
classified indefinitely

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.710
in an attempt to increase
government transparency.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.120
The order also provides deadlines

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.260
for declassifying information

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.770
exempted from automatic
declassification at 25 years.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.210
The National Declassification Center

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.070
is part of the National Archives.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.830
Now, Trump did not rescind
Obama's executive order,

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.090
and that means that government departments

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.070
are still following its procedures.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.810
Trump himself was probably not obligated

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.330
to follow this order when
declassifying records,

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:31.920
although some lawyers have argued that

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.510
Trump was legally required to

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.980
follow the order or rescind it.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.660
Now, courts have never
had to decide this issue

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.250
because presidents who don't agree with

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.540
their predecessor's orders

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.000
simply rescind them and issue new ones,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.700
but, the argument goes,
because Trump was president

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.890
and had plenary powers
to declassify things,

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.860
he could just declassify
documents without telling anyone.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.676
Oh, yes, the Vulcan mind declassification.

00:03:52.676 --> 00:03:54.870
- I know what you know.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.640
- Now, during his administration,

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.350
Trump asserted that he
could declassify records

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.060
by word, tweet, or thought.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.767
For example, on October 6th,
2020, Trump tweeted, quote,

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:05.340
"I have fully authorized
the total declassification

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.140
of any and all documents pertaining to

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.020
the single greatest political
crime in American history,

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.280
the Russia hoax.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.850
Likewise, the Hillary Clinton
email scandal. No redactions!"

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:16.200
Exclamation point.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.390
Now, news organizations rushed to obtain

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.430
the apparently declassified documents,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.050
but the Trump White House denied that

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:23.130
the president had the authority

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.370
to declassify records by tweet
without following protocol.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.620
White House chief of staff
Mark Meadows said that

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.750
Trump's tweets about
declassifying documents

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.870
related to the Russia
investigation were not an order

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:37.260
to declassify or release
further documents.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.247
He filed a sworn
declaration stating, quote,

00:04:39.247 --> 00:04:40.740
"The President indicated to me that

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:41.820
his statements on Twitter

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.310
were not self-executing
declassification orders

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.680
and do not require the
declassification or release

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.600
of any particular documents."

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.360
Trump's defenders claim that
he declassified material

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.890
simply by speaking the words aloud,

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.090
and that the real problem is that

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:56.370
other people didn't follow
the appropriate processes

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:57.720
after Trump spoke.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.400
Former Pentagon employee Kashyap Patel

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.330
said that he was in the room

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.330
when Trump said that they were
declassifying information.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.700
Patel then threw White
House lawyers under the bus.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.250
Quote, "White House counsel
failed to generate the paperwork

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.200
to change the classification markings,

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.720
but that doesn't mean the
information wasn't declassified."

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.310
Now, when a document is declassified,

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:15.930
it's stamped with the word "declassified"

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.760
and the date that the
records were declassified.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:18.593
If that didn't happen,

00:05:18.593 --> 00:05:20.010
the records haven't been declassified,

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:23.040
or at least they must be
treated as still classified.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.230
Now, none of that has
stopped Trump's defenders

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.210
from claiming that Trump's
declassification powers

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:28.043
were so broad

00:05:28.043 --> 00:05:30.510
that he could actually
declassify stuff with his mind.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.887
A spokesperson for the Heritage
Foundation said, quote,

00:05:32.887 --> 00:05:33.990
"There's a rich debate about

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:35.850
whether or non a document is declassified

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.290
if the president has decided,

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.720
but not communicated it
outside of his own head."

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.803
Now, maybe there is a debate
at the Heritage Foundation,

00:05:42.803 --> 00:05:46.140
but that defense would probably
not work in a court of law

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.090
to suggest that the president's thoughts

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.560
have the power of law.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.230
Now, bottom line, if the
allegedly declassified documents

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.810
weren't modified to state that
they had been declassified,

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.850
then they hadn't been declassified.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.620
Now, Trump's defenders
have also claimed that

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.450
the president can't break any laws

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.220
because the president is
literally above the law

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.750
when it comes to this situation.

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:05.640
Fox News laid out this theory succinctly.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.777
- Famously, President Nixon said,

00:06:07.777 --> 00:06:10.560
"If the president does it,
then it is not illegal."

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:11.850
- Ah, yes, Richard Nixon,

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.327
famously accurate arbiter of legality.

00:06:14.327 --> 00:06:17.228
Now, here's the backstory
to this particular claim.

00:06:17.228 --> 00:06:20.370
In a series of interviews
with the BBC's David Frost,

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:23.130
former president Richard Nixon
argues that the constitution

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:25.020
allows the president to break the law.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.760
When Frost pressed Nixon

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.400
on authorizing the burglary
of the Watergate Hotel,

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.380
which he knew was illegal, Nixon said-

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.550
- [Nixon] Well, when
the president does it,

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.130
that means that it is not illegal.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:35.963
- [Frost] By definition?

00:06:35.963 --> 00:06:37.230
- [Nixon] Exactly.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.110
Then the president's
decision in that instance

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:43.901
is one that enables those who carry it out

00:06:43.901 --> 00:06:46.740
to carry it out without violating a law.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.624
- Nixon and Trump shared a
theory of the unitarian executive

00:06:49.624 --> 00:06:52.110
that placed no limits on
the president's power.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:53.550
Trump claimed that, as president,

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.770
the constitution vested
him with total authority.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:57.750
- Then I have an article too

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:00.510
where I have the right to do
whatever I want as president,

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.390
but I don't even talk about that.

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:05.400
- The unitary executive was originally

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:06.900
a limited thesis about
the president's power

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.750
to remove upper-level
executive branch officials,

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:12.840
but, over many years, the
theory has expanded in scope.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.910
Presidents invoke the
unitary executive theory

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.772
whenever they want more expansive power,

00:07:16.772 --> 00:07:19.350
but in 2020, the Supreme
Court ruled against

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.090
Trump's version of the
unitary executive theory

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.100
in a case where he
challenged New York's right

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:24.990
to subpoena Trump and
the Trump organization

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.670
for alleged financial crimes.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.790
In Trump v. Vance, the court
rejected the proposition that

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:32.670
a sitting president can use their powers

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:34.290
under Article 2 of the constitution

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.170
to assert immunity from
criminal investigation.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.840
The majority opinion also
rejected Trump's alternate theory

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.336
that prosecutors should make
a heightened showing of need

00:07:42.336 --> 00:07:45.420
before issuing a subpoena
to the president.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:46.982
Then, in Trump versus Mazars,

00:07:46.982 --> 00:07:48.270
the Supreme Court ruled that

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:50.940
the president wasn't immune
from congressional subpoenas.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.260
Now, in Mazars, the court ruled that

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:53.670
Congress had not established a purpose

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:54.870
for demanding the Trump records,

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:56.610
while, in Vance, the court ordered Trump

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.220
to respond to the subpoena for
the same financial records.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:00.360
However, both of these cases

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.130
rejected the special needs standard

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:04.740
for issuing a criminal
or congressional subpoena

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.350
to a president, and the
Vance and Mazars cases

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.300
are in line with the Supreme
Court's other decisions

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:11.100
on the limits of presidential power.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.020
In US v. Nixon, the court ruled that

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.300
Richard Nixon had to turn
over White House records

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.350
to a special prosecutor,

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:17.700
and in Clinton v. Jones,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.920
the court also held that the
president can be sued civilly

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:22.050
for private conduct before he took office.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:23.340
Still, the counterargument is that

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.049
the president has plenary authority

00:08:25.049 --> 00:08:28.155
over classification and
declassification of documents.

00:08:28.155 --> 00:08:30.327
If a sitting president were to decide

00:08:30.327 --> 00:08:33.190
to declassify every single record

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:35.040
in the United States government,

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:36.930
the president has that power.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:39.210
It's just a matter of telling people

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:41.700
and making sure that they
follow the process to do so.

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.260
Those such arguments are
probably not gonna help

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.960
a former president who is
no longer the president,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:46.950
but what about the claims of

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:49.890
attorney-client privilege
and executive privilege?

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.420
Well, Trump also claims that

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:53.010
the gathered documents are protected

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:55.410
by various kinds of privilege.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:58.777
He wrote in some sort of tweet-like,

00:08:58.777 --> 00:08:59.610
"Oh, great!

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.140
It has just been learned that the FBI,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.240
in its now famous raid of Mar-a-Lago,

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.420
took boxes of privileged
attorney-client material,

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.363
and also executive privilege material,

00:09:08.363 --> 00:09:11.100
which they knowingly
should not have taken.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:13.290
By copy of this truth, I
respectfully request that

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:14.970
these documents be immediately returned to

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:16.980
the location from which they were taken.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:17.813
Thank you!"

00:09:17.813 --> 00:09:18.870
Exclamation point.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.610
So let's say you have a friend sitting in

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:21.930
on a meeting with your lawyer,

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:26.580
or you CC your dad on
an email to your lawyer.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.160
Well, you have then waived
the attorney-client privilege,

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:30.600
and when can that privilege be raised?

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:33.750
Well, when someone makes
a legal demand for things

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.630
that involve communications
between a lawyer and a client.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:37.890
This could be made through

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:40.110
a discovery request during litigation

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.870
or through a subpoena for
documents served on a lawyer,

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:43.860
or it could be made by

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.530
someone requesting that a
lawyer testify under oath.

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:49.740
The client is the person who
is said to hold the privilege.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.480
That means that the client can waive

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:53.850
the attorney-client
privilege as a defense,

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:55.740
but a lawyer cannot waive the privilege

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:57.180
without the client's consent,

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.340
and the person claiming
privilege has the burden of

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.770
proving that they are
entitled to that privilege.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.870
Now, here, Trump has been
making a privilege argument

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.910
to assert that the FBI took material

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:08.520
that was covered by the
attorney-client privilege,

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:11.400
and it's possible that the
investigators removed documents

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:12.840
that are covered by the privilege.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:15.750
For example, we know that
the FBI collected and took

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.790
some of Trump's passports,

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:20.250
though they did return
that shortly thereafter,

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.070
and that's because, when officers
execute a search warrant,

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:25.590
they don't read every page of
every document that they find.

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:28.356
That could take days or
even months or years.

00:10:28.356 --> 00:10:30.390
Instead, they quickly identify material

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:33.840
that fits within the warrant's
specifics and then remove it.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.120
Then, a team of lawyers and investigators

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:37.743
go through the material in detail.

00:10:37.743 --> 00:10:39.563
The Wall Street Journal reported that

00:10:39.563 --> 00:10:42.090
the DOJ has already set up a filter team,

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:45.510
also known as a taint team,
to review the seized materials

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.430
before they are viewed by investigators

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:49.230
to determine whether
any of those documents

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.260
are indeed protected by
attorney-client privilege.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.610
Now, attorney-client privilege is tricky

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:54.443
when you're talking about

00:10:54.443 --> 00:10:57.030
advice that the president
received as president.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:57.990
Discussions between, say,

00:10:57.990 --> 00:10:59.820
the president and the attorney general

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:02.280
are not shielded by the
attorney-client privilege.

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:04.800
The attorney general is
not the president's lawyer.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:06.810
Many of these relationships
in the executive branch

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:07.980
are effectively between

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.200
the office of the president
and the attorneys,

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.620
not specifically the president
as a person individually,

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.690
which is why things like
executive privilege exists,

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:17.850
which we'll talk about in just a second,

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:18.960
but, by the same token,

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:22.170
presidents do hire lawyers
in their individual capacity,

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:24.840
and that's when attorney-client
privilege would attach.

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.150
Think Clinton during the Lewinsky scandal

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.100
or Trump and Giuliani.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:30.030
I mean, I guess.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:30.930
Did we ever figure out

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:34.113
if he was actually Trump's
individual attorney?

00:11:35.082 --> 00:11:37.140
We'll have to get back
to that, but, anyway,

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:38.040
attorney-client privilege

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.690
would attach to those communications

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:42.900
between the individual
who is the president

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.761
and their individual lawyer,

00:11:44.761 --> 00:11:46.800
and also the work product doctrine

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:49.440
would protect documents
created in those relationships,

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:51.060
but if some of the documents

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.160
are covered by the
attorney-client privilege,

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.230
one would assume that
that is mutually exclusive

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:57.060
to those documents
belonging to the government

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.930
and needing to return to
the government as well.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:03.690
But, in a weird twist,
it's the exact opposite

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:06.000
for the other privilege
that Trump asserts.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.190
Trump also asserts executive privilege

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:09.960
over the taken documents.

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:10.860
He seems to believe that

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:12.720
all of the records taken during the search

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:13.890
were inappropriately removed

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:16.260
on the basis of executive privilege.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.110
Apparently, Trump's theory
of executive privilege is

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:20.340
everything I want to keep secret

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:23.280
is therefore secret in perpetuity forever,

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.920
but that has never
matched what the law says.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.330
Most legal scholars conclude that,

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.700
since the privilege of executive privilege

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:31.800
attaches to the office of the presidency,

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.950
rather than the person who
is occupying that office,

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:37.260
a current president can decide
whether his predecessors

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:39.390
can invoke executive privilege or not.

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:40.980
A former president might be able to

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:42.840
assert executive privilege,

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:44.310
but it would be up to
the current president

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.290
to uphold that assertion or not.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:47.760
The DOJ would probably argue that,

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:50.310
even if some of the documents
seized because the warrant

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:51.960
are covered by executive privilege,

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:54.973
all the documents are legally
property of the government,

00:12:54.973 --> 00:12:57.210
but since executive privilege attaches to

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:58.590
the executive branch documents

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:01.110
and specifically the current office holder

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:03.810
of the president of the
United States, it seems like,

00:13:03.810 --> 00:13:07.281
if Trump possesses any
executive privilege documents,

00:13:07.281 --> 00:13:09.780
then Trump necessarily is in violation of

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:11.040
the statutes that we've talked about.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.890
In other words, if executive
privilege is in play,

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.720
which it doesn't seem like it should be,

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:18.150
because Trump isn't in office anymore,

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.700
it seems like Trump shouldn't
have any of those documents.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.950
So, claims of executive privilege

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:24.450
might actually be a confession.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:27.660
- We did what we came here to do.

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:28.890
- Generally, former presidents

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:30.810
do not have a right to
keep documents secret

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.700
or take them home from the White House

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:34.470
because of executive privilege.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:35.820
Executive privilege accrues

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:37.950
to the benefit of the
office of the president.

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:40.110
Some of the issues surrounding
executive privilege

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:41.160
were litigated in a case called

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.620
Nixon v. the Administrator
of General Services.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:44.940
When Richard Nixon resigned,

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:46.830
he left behind millions
of pages of documents

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.696
and many audio recordings
of his time in office.

00:13:49.696 --> 00:13:50.850
He had an agreement with

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:52.860
the administrator of General Services

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:55.425
to store these materials
near his California home.

00:13:55.425 --> 00:13:57.000
This agreement also specified that

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.700
Nixon could destroy some of the
materials at his discretion,

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:01.800
but Congress passed the
Presidential Records Act

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:03.270
in part to stop Nixon

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:05.220
from destroying these important records,

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.310
and Nixon challenged the
constitutionality of the PRA.

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:10.410
The Supreme Court,
however, upheld the act.

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.090
The court held that it was constitutional

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:13.470
to give custody of the materials

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:15.930
to officials who worked
in the executive branch,

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:17.910
and that these materials
could only be released

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:19.770
if that action was not barred

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:22.260
by some applicable executive privilege.

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:24.120
Now, with respect to executive privilege,

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:25.950
the Supreme Court rejected Nixon's claim

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:28.170
that presidential
privilege barred archivists

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:30.390
from scrutinizing the materials.

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.490
Nixon raised First Amendment
and privacy concerns

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:35.010
about what was in the
materials from his presidency,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:35.910
but the court held that

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:38.820
the intrusions into Nixon's
private life were minimal

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:41.790
and any intrusion was outweighed
by a clear public interest

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:42.900
in preserving materials

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.690
for legitimate historical
and governmental purposes.

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:48.450
Now, here, Trump seems to be
asserting executive privilege

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:50.514
over the materials that
were stored in Mar-a-Lago,

00:14:50.514 --> 00:14:52.740
but Trump probably doesn't have a right

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.500
to claim executive privilege
as an ex-president.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.600
That privilege is held
by the current president

00:14:57.600 --> 00:14:59.769
per Nixon v. GSA.

00:14:59.769 --> 00:15:02.432
In that Nixon case, the
Supreme Court held that, quote,

00:15:02.432 --> 00:15:05.190
"Executive privilege is
not for the benefit of

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:06.660
the president as an individual,

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.520
but for the benefit of the Republic."

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.027
The Supreme Court recognized, quote,

00:15:10.027 --> 00:15:12.120
"The presumptive confidentiality

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.720
of presidential communications
as a qualified privilege,"

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:18.577
That privilege is necessary
because a president, quote,

00:15:18.577 --> 00:15:19.830
"And those who assist him

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:21.840
must be free to explore alternatives

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:24.650
in the process of shaping
policies and making decisions

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:26.379
and to do so in a way

00:15:26.379 --> 00:15:29.640
many would be unwilling to
express except privately."

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.320
The court concluded
that executive privilege

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.080
could hypothetically be
asserted by a former president,

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.300
but only as to materials
whose content falls within

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:37.207
the scope of, quote,

00:15:37.207 --> 00:15:38.873
"Communications in performance of

00:15:38.873 --> 00:15:41.040
a president's responsibilities
of his office,

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:42.540
and made in the process of

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:44.760
shaping policies and making decisions."

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:46.050
So far, no former president

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:47.490
has been able to meet this standard,

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:50.790
since it emphasizes the
materials had to have been made

00:15:50.790 --> 00:15:52.320
while the person was president

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.510
in the process of making
presidential decisions,

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.550
and the same principle
applies to materials created

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.980
before the president is
sworn in as president.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:00.570
Trump's allies tried to enlarge

00:16:00.570 --> 00:16:02.040
the scope of executive privilege

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.230
so that it applied to the president-elect,

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:05.700
but at least a district
court ruling said that

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:08.010
executive privilege may not be
asserted by a president-elect

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:10.170
over communications made
before he takes office.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.450
So, with the background on
Trump's defenses out of the way,

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:14.190
let's look at the new developments.

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:15.420
The first big development is that

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:17.817
Trump finally filed something in court.

00:16:17.817 --> 00:16:20.160
Trump's legal team did not file anything

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:21.270
challenging the search warrant

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:22.800
for two weeks after the search,

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.037
and this is really inexcusable.

00:16:25.037 --> 00:16:27.300
If law enforcement takes materials

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:29.010
that are protected by
attorney-client privilege

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:31.710
or are a lawyer's work product,
that's an urgent matter.

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:33.360
That should be challenged within days

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:35.241
or even hours of the search.

00:16:35.241 --> 00:16:36.450
When Trump's legal team

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:38.430
got around to filing something, however,

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:41.670
they chose to file a motion
to appoint a special master.

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:42.960
Now, that's not a crazy request,

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.600
as governed by federal
rule of Civil Procedure 53,

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:46.433
which provides that

00:16:46.433 --> 00:16:48.480
the court in which any action is pending

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:50.280
may appoint a special master therein,

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:52.440
and that a reference to a special master

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:55.140
shall be the exception, not the rule.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:56.790
Now, the traditional role
of the special master

00:16:56.790 --> 00:16:58.140
is to hold evidentiary hearings

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:00.630
and make rulings that
will facilitate a trial

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:02.460
in the court where the matter is pending.

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:03.720
They're used to assist the judge

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:06.060
in making discovery rulings,
facilitating settlements,

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:07.650
supervising electronic discovery,

00:17:07.650 --> 00:17:10.170
reviewing documents and data
that might be privileged.

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:11.580
Now, a special master in the Trump case

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:13.200
could be appointed to
review all the material

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:14.520
that was removed from Mar-a-Lago

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:16.731
to determine if any of
it is indeed privileged.

00:17:16.731 --> 00:17:19.290
That's not unusual for a special master

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:21.534
to be appointed to assist
with that litigation,

00:17:21.534 --> 00:17:24.780
but it's not clear what value
that would have for Trump.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:27.720
He's not really challenging
the warrant itself.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:29.640
Now, before we get to the
substance of this motion,

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:33.771
I should point out that it's
just terribly, terribly written

00:17:33.771 --> 00:17:36.900
in the very basics of the motion.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.240
Trump's lawyers filed
it as a separate case,

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:40.770
which appears to be wrong,

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:43.230
which is why the clerks
docketed it as a complaint,

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:45.372
but it's not a complaint
in the traditional sense.

00:17:45.372 --> 00:17:46.800
The motion doesn't include

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:49.244
a statement of facts or any evidence,

00:17:49.244 --> 00:17:52.259
and they should have
attached a declaration

00:17:52.259 --> 00:17:55.830
or a sworn statement from
Trump or Trump's lawyers

00:17:55.830 --> 00:17:58.620
attesting to the facts
that they attest to.

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:00.180
They could have provided
plenty of evidence

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:01.770
to support their case if it exists,

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:02.603
like, for example,

00:18:02.603 --> 00:18:05.790
the videotapes of the
FBI searching Mar-a-Lago,

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:06.623
but they just didn't.

00:18:06.623 --> 00:18:07.950
It only attaches a couple of exhibits

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:10.384
related to the warrant itself,
and the motion also ignores

00:18:10.384 --> 00:18:12.660
some of the DOJ's strongest facts

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:14.850
that Trump's lawyer signed
a sworn statement in June,

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.070
claiming that they had
returned all of the documents.

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:18.690
Trump could have filed an affidavit

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:19.920
saying that he personally believed

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:22.560
he had returned all of the
records, or that Christina Bobb

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:24.210
didn't have the authority
to speak for him,

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:28.020
or that he had no idea that
he had to return the documents

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:30.510
or something to refute his
own lawyer's statements,

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:32.460
but, instead, the motion
just quotes commentary

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.560
that Trump posted on Truth Social,

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.510
but these assertions are not the same as

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:38.760
swearing to facts under
the penalty of perjury,

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:41.220
which is normally what you
would do in a motion like this,

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:43.440
but perhaps the most incredible
thing about this motion is

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:44.850
that it appears to admit that

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:46.473
Trump committed the crimes at issue.

00:18:46.473 --> 00:18:49.020
After chronicling the
timeline of the back and forth

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:50.190
between Trump and the government,

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.650
and how Trump and his
team did a diligent search

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:54.630
for classified documents
and turned them over,

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:57.330
he then admits that, when
the counterintelligence team

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:00.303
at the DOJ's National Security
Division came back in June,

00:19:00.303 --> 00:19:02.692
Trump gave them apparently
more classified documents

00:19:02.692 --> 00:19:04.990
that they had withheld before.

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:08.010
The motion, in a truly
spectacular use of passive voice,

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:10.200
states, quote, "Responsive documents

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:12.330
were provided to the FBI agents."

00:19:12.330 --> 00:19:13.830
And despite Trump's vague assertions

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.420
that he cooperated with the government,

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:17.640
the bottom line is that these
records were sitting in boxes

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:19.110
at the basement of a private club,

00:19:19.110 --> 00:19:20.670
and they belonged to NARA,

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:22.890
and Trump wouldn't give them back,

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:25.170
and it looks more and
more like the DOJ and NARA

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:28.020
bent over backwards with Trump
on getting the records back,

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:29.820
and they only applied for a search warrant

00:19:29.820 --> 00:19:32.070
when he refused to return
the government property,

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:34.110
and then, of course,
there's Trump's new lawyers.

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:36.720
Trump seems to find himself
in a familiar situation,

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:38.580
hiring new lawyers without much vetting,

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:40.740
and then watching the lawyers
make careless mistakes.

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:42.540
For example, the motion to
appoint a special master

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:43.920
was signed by three lawyers,

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:46.320
a Florida license insurance
lawyer, Lindsay Halligan,

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:48.240
plus Jim Trusty from Washington, DC,

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:49.860
and Evan Corcoran from Baltimore.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:51.270
Now, in the US, lawyers are licensed

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:52.650
to practice in specific jurisdictions,

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.450
so if a lawyer wants to represent a client

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:56.292
in a state where he or
she is not licensed,

00:19:56.292 --> 00:19:58.020
they have to file a motion with the court,

00:19:58.020 --> 00:19:59.160
asking them to be admitted.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:00.930
This is a very straightforward process

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:02.010
known as pro hoc vice.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:03.510
Now, this is a very easy motion.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:04.740
You basically just fill out a form

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:06.270
and say that you're in good standing

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:07.800
in the places that you practice,

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:09.930
but Trusty and Corcoran failed to do this.

00:20:09.930 --> 00:20:11.190
The court rejected their motions

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:13.267
and issued a paperless order
on the docket stating that,

00:20:13.267 --> 00:20:15.480
"Local counsel is instructed
to refile the motions

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:17.250
in strict accordance with Local Rule 4(b)

00:20:17.250 --> 00:20:19.500
of the Rules Governing
the Admission, Practice,

00:20:19.500 --> 00:20:21.420
Peer Review, and Discipline of Attorneys.

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:23.790
A sample motion can be found
on the Court's website."

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.140
At that URL.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:26.790
The motions were denied without prejudice,

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:29.220
but it's really just
really careless mistakes.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:31.590
But going back to the motion
to appoint a special master,

00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:33.150
despite Trump's social media claims

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:34.320
that his team would be filing

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:35.940
a major Fourth amendment motion,

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.640
the lawyers did not file a
motion to suppress the warrant,

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:40.590
and the special master
doesn't have the authority

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.390
to throw the warrant out,
and since it looks like

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.040
Trump's team filed this
in the wrong court,

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:45.630
instead of in the same case

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:47.370
that gave rise to the search warrant,

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:49.922
the new court wasted no time
in responding to the motion.

00:20:49.922 --> 00:20:51.770
The judge actually ordered
Trump's team to, quote,

00:20:51.770 --> 00:20:54.030
"File a supplement to the Motion

00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:55.470
further elaborating on the following.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:56.580
One, the asserted basis

00:20:56.580 --> 00:20:58.560
for the exercise of this
Court's jurisdiction,

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.810
whether legal,
equitable/anomalous, or both.

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:03.030
Two, the framework applicable
to the court's jurisdiction.

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:04.590
Three, the precise relief sought,

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:06.390
including any request
for injunctive relief,

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:07.890
pending resolution of the Motion.

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:09.270
Four, the effect, if any,

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:11.880
of the proceeding before
Magistrate Bruce E. Reinhart.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.917
And, five, the status of
the plaintiff's efforts

00:21:13.917 --> 00:21:16.350
to perfect service on defendants."

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:17.670
Basically, that's the court's way of being

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:20.130
extremely skeptical that they
filed in the right court,

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:22.320
that this court has any
power to do anything,

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:23.730
and basically suggesting that

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:26.343
they should go back in
front of Magistrate Reinhart

00:21:26.343 --> 00:21:28.620
to deal with this particular issue.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.510
Now, the second big development
is that John Solomon,

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:32.490
the man Trump named as his
personal representative

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:33.780
with the National Archives,

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:36.450
released a letter from the
National Archives to Trump.

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:38.233
Now, I'm gonna assume this letter is real,

00:21:38.233 --> 00:21:40.050
but, so far, it hasn't been validated,

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:42.960
but, also, I have no idea
why this was released,

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:45.690
because it's incredibly
damaging to Trump's claims.

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:48.720
The archivist quoted the
applicable parts of the Nixon case

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:50.910
to explain why it was
so important for NARA

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:52.710
to have custody of the records.

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:54.750
Quote, "The Supreme
Court specifically noted

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:55.980
that an incumbent president

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:58.230
should not be dependent on happenstance

00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:00.088
or the whim of a prior president

00:22:00.088 --> 00:22:03.030
when he seeks access to
records of past decisions

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:06.000
that define or channel current
governmental obligations."

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:06.870
And the letter states that

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:08.280
Trump is blocking the National Archives

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.470
from taking boxes of
national security information

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:13.410
to the DOJ and to the
Intelligence Community

00:22:13.410 --> 00:22:15.439
so that they can assess what information

00:22:15.439 --> 00:22:18.150
might have been revealed to third parties.

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:20.460
The letter says that these
officials need to take steps

00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:23.460
to mitigate the harms from
mishandling the material.

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:25.410
Essentially, the archivist is saying that

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.360
it's urgently important
for government officials

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:28.530
to do a damage assessment,

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:30.390
because some of its intelligence sources

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:32.160
could have been burned by Trump.

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:33.180
The letter tells Trump that

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:35.670
the incumbent president,
Biden, needs the records

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:37.860
and that the DOJ had already
explained all of this to him.

00:22:37.860 --> 00:22:40.110
Quote, "There are important
national security interests

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:42.450
in the FBI and others in
the Intelligence Community

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:44.190
getting access to these materials.

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:46.560
According to NARA, among
the materials in the boxes

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:48.846
are over 100 documents with
classification markings,

00:22:48.846 --> 00:22:51.540
comprising more than 700 pages.

00:22:51.540 --> 00:22:53.850
Some include the highest
levels of classification,

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:56.490
including Special Access
Programs SAP materials.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:59.130
Access to the materials
is not only necessary

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:01.612
for purposes of our ongoing
criminal investigation,

00:23:01.612 --> 00:23:03.990
but the executive branch must also conduct

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:05.400
an assessment of the potential damage

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:07.230
resulting from the apparent manner

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:09.750
in which these materials
were stored and transported

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.420
and take any necessary remedial steps.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:14.010
The National Archives
originally wanted them

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:15.930
by the end of April 2022,

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:17.970
but Trump sent NARA a
letter on April 29th,

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:19.710
informing NARA that, in his opinion,

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:21.679
they were covered by executive privilege.

00:23:21.679 --> 00:23:24.090
This delayed the process
for an additional month.

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:26.460
NARA rejected the assertion
of privilege though.

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:28.890
The archivist consulted with
the assistant attorney general

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:30.727
for the Office of Legal
Counsel, which, quote,

00:23:30.727 --> 00:23:32.940
"Advised me that there is no precedent

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:34.410
for an assertion of executive privilege

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:36.776
by a former president against
an incumbent president

00:23:36.776 --> 00:23:39.420
to prevent the latter
from obtaining from NARA

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:42.360
presidential records belonging
to the federal government

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:44.040
where such records contain information

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:45.990
that is needed for the conduct of

00:23:45.990 --> 00:23:48.360
current business of the
incumbent president's office

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:50.490
and that is not otherwise available."

00:23:50.490 --> 00:23:53.310
So, assuming this letter is
accurate, it's just insane that

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:55.350
Trump did not turn over
all of the documents

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:56.610
months and months ago,

00:23:56.610 --> 00:23:57.780
and then the final development is

00:23:57.780 --> 00:23:59.370
that parts of the affidavit

00:23:59.370 --> 00:24:01.440
that gave rise to the
search warrant are unsealed.

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:04.470
While the Trump said that he
wanted the affidavit unsealed,

00:24:04.470 --> 00:24:06.420
he actually didn't join or do anything

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:09.390
with respect to the motion
to unseal the search warrant,

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:10.962
but despite that, several
news organizations

00:24:10.962 --> 00:24:13.080
did move forward with the motion,

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:14.940
and the magistrate judge ruled in favor of

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:17.400
unsealing parts of the
affidavit with redactions.

00:24:17.400 --> 00:24:18.840
Quote, "As I ruled from the bench

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:19.950
at the conclusion of the hearing,

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:21.570
I find that, on the present record,

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:23.010
the government has not met its burden of

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:25.620
showing that the entire
affidavit should remain sealed."

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:26.790
Generally, affidavits like these

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:29.280
are not revealed until an
indictment has been filed,

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:31.350
because it could impact an investigation,

00:24:31.350 --> 00:24:32.183
but, generally speaking,

00:24:32.183 --> 00:24:34.590
there's a presumption of
validity of these affidavits,

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:35.423
but that doesn't mean that

00:24:35.423 --> 00:24:36.780
a defendant can't challenge the warrant

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:40.320
if it's based on deliberate
falsehoods or other wrongdoing,

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:42.660
and if a search warrant
is defective or deficient,

00:24:42.660 --> 00:24:44.370
a criminal defendant can file a motion

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:46.353
to suppress evidence
from being used at trial,

00:24:46.353 --> 00:24:49.320
but since Trump has not
been charged with a crime,

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.990
filing a motion to suppress
is not yet an option,

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:54.060
though it might be in the
future. But if you'd like to hear

